<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hyeja</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 595–615</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hyeja was a Buddhist monk from the Korean kingdom of Goguryeo who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 595. According to the Nihon shoki he became the teacher of Prince Shotoku, the regent of Empress Suiko, and helped to establish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamato-court">Yamato court</a>. He returned to Goguryeo in 615, and his career illustrates the role of Korean monks in bringing Buddhist learning to Japan.</p></div>
